WEBVTT

00:00:00.612 --> 00:00:05.652
Subqueries become useful when one part of the question depends on the result of another query.

00:00:05.652 --> 00:00:10.142
Sometimes the inner query produces one benchmark such as average age.

00:00:10.142 --> 00:00:12.972
Sometimes it produces a set of patient IDs.

00:00:12.972 --> 00:00:16.872
Sometimes we only need to know whether a matching related row exists.

00:00:16.872 --> 00:00:22.392
And sometimes the inner query depends on the current outer row.

00:00:22.392 --> 00:00:26.487
These are different contracts, and recognizing the contract is more useful than memorizing syntax.

00:00:26.487 --> 00:00:31.567
Connect this idea to an e-commerce team finding customers who purchased a target product.

00:00:31.567 --> 00:00:36.867
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:36.867 --> 00:00:43.937
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:43.937 --> 00:00:48.887
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:48.887 --> 00:00:53.760
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
